	Pilot decision criteria
Measure four layers separately.
Capture quality

Track median capture time, manual fields required, corrections and abandonment.
State quality

This is arguably more important than raw notification engagement.
Action quality

“Opened notification” should not count as product success.
Outcome quality
For food:

Use the same measurement protocol before and during the intervention. Do not set “40%” as a validated target merely because one CozZo intervention reported 43%; TotalCtrl illustrates that outcome effects are not automatically reproducible. [19]
For a cross-domain product decision, I would use the following project decision gates, explicitly as design thresholds rather than literature-derived truths:
* At least 80% critical-task completion in the prototype without moderator rescue.
* Median assisted capture under roughly 30 seconds for a normal item, or clear evidence users accept longer capture for high-value/safety-critical items.
* A materially lower stale-record rate than a manual-list baseline.
* The same shared Needs Attention → Meaning → Action → Resolve model is understood without redesign in at least food + cosmetics.
* Participants can correctly distinguish that food, cosmetics, medicine and warranty do not share the same meaning of “expired.”
* No audited safety-critical medicine recommendation is generated without an authoritative source.
The most important cross-domain success criterion is qualitative:
Does the common abstraction reduce cognitive effort, or force users to learn an abstract lifecycle-management system they never asked for?
